WEBVTT

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.300
ABINAV RAMEESH: Thank you all
for joining my session today.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.800
My name is Abinav Rameesh.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.090
I am a Product Manager on
the Azure DocumentDB team.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.710
And today I'm going
to talk to you all

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.590
about how we built
Azure DocumentDB

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.930
on opensource foundations.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:20.910
Essentially, the theme of
this conversation is going

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.820
to be how we started
with a managed service,

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.270
went back into building
an opensource engine

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.700
from the managed service,
but then funneled all

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.060
of our opensource learnings
from the project back

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.980
into the managed service.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.380
So hope you all enjoy this talk.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.020
For a little bit about who we
are and what we do as a product,

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.220
we are an opensource,
fully MongoDB compatible,

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.280
NoSQL document database.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.870
We are Postgres-backed,

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.660
and that's where the
major fundamentals

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.990
of our opensource
fundamentals kick in.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:57.300
We are obviously
fully opensource,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.220
and what that means
is we are also able

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.740
to provide multicloud
functionality.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.810
And multicloud here
means several things.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:07.410
It could actually be across
multiple cloud providers,

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.840
but it could also be
a hybrid cloud setup,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.970
where you're running
with one foot on-prem

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.310
and one foot on Azure and so on.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:15.630
And of course, at
the end of the day,

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.770
we did build a managed service
around this foundation.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.800
And so what you do get with
Azure DocumentDB is all

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.890
of the managed bells and
whistles, the knobs and levers

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.190
that come with being
built around that engine

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:32.730
in the opensource, but
fully managed in Azure.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:36.060
I wanted to start a little
bit about what our journey

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.790
to opensource
actually looked like.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:42.990
And this is a very high-level
view of what that one

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.850
and a half year process,
actually a little bit more

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:48.930
than that, ended up entailing.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:52.620
And as I called out, we actually
started with Azure DocumentDB.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.000
We were called
something before that.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.610
This was before January 2024.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:57.390
We went GA.

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:00.180
We had the managed service,
but then what we thought was

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:04.020
because we are already built
on an opensource database,

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.050
why not opensource
our product in itself

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.510
and have an opensource project

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:12.300
that people can actually look
at, see what we're building,

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.600
see how we're doing it, and
bring the developer community

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.900
into that building process.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.510
So in January 2024,
we began thinking

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:25.110
about what opensource
would mean for us.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:28.230
And interestingly, the name
DocumentDB actually showed

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:31.440
up in the opensource project
before we even started calling

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:32.700
ourselves that.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.490
That's a whole different story.

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:35.840
But that started a little

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.160
over two years ago the
entire conversation for us.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.230
And obviously being
part of Microsoft,

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:44.240
there were several
opensource projects

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.670
that we had already
heard of, several teams

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:49.050
that had built
around opensource.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.150
And so we got a lot of advice.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:54.000
And because of our
background in Postgres,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.750
and because of Citus having
been acquired in 2019,

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:02.520
and having built a scale-out
Postgres database of their own,

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.360
we got a lot of insight and
feedback even about the approach

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.720
that we were starting to
take, the right mindset,

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.590
the right perspective to have
on really building truly

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.690
with an opensource engine,
and then putting all

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:19.080
of the managed
components around it.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:20.940
And their recommendation,
which is something

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.740
that took us a while to get to,

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:26.160
was essentially having a very
clear idea if we're looking

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.310
to build around opensource on
what the project should look

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.190
like and what the product
should look like.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:33.570
And I'll actually get
into a little bit

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:36.360
about what this distinction
meant, but this was something

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.430
that we had no idea about.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:41.100
And this was a huge learning
for us and actually continues

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:44.280
to be a learning even as we
look well beyond this journey

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.310
and clearly have our feet wet

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:51.180
in both the project
and the product.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.310
What came very quickly after
that was of course figuring

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:56.250
out what the licensing
model should look like.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.440
And obviously, because
we're built in Azure,

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.440
because we are a
subscription-based service,

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.940
we certainly don't
have any licenses

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:04.740
for the managed product.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:05.490
But at the end of the day,

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:08.340
opensource does entail
having a license.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.260
It could actually entail
having multiple licenses,

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.990
depending on the nature
of your project.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:15.300
And we actually had to have
long, lengthy conversations

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.510
around what is the best license

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:21.930
that the opensource community
could actually consume.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.720
And so we landed on the MIT
license, and that was actually

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.150
because we wanted
this to be as simple

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.520
and as frictionless as possible.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:30.570
For anyone familiar with this,

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.730
the MIT license is
three paragraphs.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.710
There's absolutely
no restrictions

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.930
on a user, on a contributor.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:39.780
You can take the project, do
whatever you want, run with it,

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:43.200
build around it, build on
top of it, build next to it.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.300
Essentially no
restrictions at all.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.460
And it'll take you about 10
seconds to finish reading

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:48.900
through the entire thing.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.030
And so that's essentially
what we landed on.

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:53.910
No regrets, clearly
the right decision.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:57.420
In September, we then started
having legal discussions,

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.940
because obviously we're talking
about a product that's part

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:02.430
of the Microsoft
ecosystem in Azure,

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:05.250
a project that's essentially
sitting independently

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:08.130
of Microsoft but still
run by Microsoft.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:09.960
So what does that
separation look

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:11.970
like of project and product?

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:13.050
And there were a lot
of things that we had

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:15.090
to learn along the way.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:17.700
But these were really more
about do's and don'ts,

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:20.520
as opposed to things that were
specific to the technical nature

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.770
of building around opensource.

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:24.690
So by this point, it's
getting close to a year.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:27.540
End of December, we said,
okay, we have to launch,

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:28.830
scramble to the finish line.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:32.280
And by Jan 2025, we made
the big announcement.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.830
DocumentDB went to opensource.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:36.870
And it was actually
very well received.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:40.440
A lot of media coverage,
a lot of press.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:44.370
A lot of adoption and all of
the typical metrics that come

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:48.390
with opensource, the GitHub
stars, the forks, the clones,

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:50.460
we saw all of that take
a very sharp rise,

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:52.590
and it was very promising.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:55.020
And a couple of months in, so
now we're looking at a little

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.750
over a year ago, there
was a good amount

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.430
of external interest as well.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:03.240
So there were a lot of people,
and I mean external companies

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.140
that had expressed verbal
support, public verbal support,

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.910
and we got up to
10 organizations

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:11.280
that said they would, on
the one hand be willing

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:16.080
to use our opensource project,
but also build on top of it

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.480
to see what they could
do just in terms

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.370
of opensource contributions.

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:23.910
And the last piece in all of
this, we had to gain true,

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:26.670
true credibility in the
opensource community.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:31.350
No better way to do that than to
join an opensource foundation.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:34.290
And that's essentially where the
Linux Foundation came into play.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:35.970
And I'll talk a little
bit about what that means

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:40.410
and of course how that ties in
to the product that's built

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:43.380
around the project as a whole.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:47.970
So the first decision that we
had to make was essentially

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:51.720
if we want to build around
an opensource project,

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.940
what is that
separation of project

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:56.100
and product going to look like?

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.980
And these are completely
different cohorts.

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:01.350
So when we think about
an opensource project,

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.390
this is really developers
that are out in the open,

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:07.260
developers that are in GitHub,
that either have projects

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:10.170
of their own that they
want a database for,

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:12.900
or looking to build
projects of their own

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:14.940
and they're looking
for a platform.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:18.690
And so the first bucket there
was essentially the user cohort.

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:22.020
And this we assumed was going
to be the biggest bucket,

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:26.250
the overwhelmingly
largest favorite bucket.

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:29.700
And what this really means
is these are people looking

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:32.400
to consume a database
for their project,

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:35.460
but not necessarily looking to
build a database of their own.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:38.370
And it's really, if you were
to think of any application,

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:40.890
anything and
everything needs data.

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:43.740
It needs data to be housed
somewhere, and obviously

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:46.440
that means having a
database of some kind.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:50.610
And so as expected, this
was the largest cohort.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:54.180
And we also were
reasonably accurate

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:57.360
in that initial assumption,
that this was going to be based

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:00.780
on enthusiasts that were
already familiar with Postgres.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:02.100
But the biggest component in all

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:04.920
of this is they wanted
a NoSQL database.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:07.620
They wanted a Mongo database.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.740
And by virtue of being
fully permissive,

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:13.770
we now provided them a free,

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:16.680
no-restrictions-based
opensource project

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.140
that was Mongo compatible.

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:24.060
The much smaller bucket, and we
assumed at least 1 in every 50,

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:29.550
hence that 98-2 split, but the
reality of it was it ended

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.580
up being a lot more
users per contributor.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:35.250
The contributors are the ones
that are actually looking

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:38.160
to build either a database of
their own and they're looking

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.980
for a platform on top of
which they want to do this.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:41.820
Which if you think about it,

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:44.330
is exactly what we were
doing on top of Postgres.

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:47.300
So Postgres is an opensource
database, has been opensource

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:51.080
for a long time, has been around
for a long time, and by virtue

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:53.990
of us building DocumentDB
on top of Postgres,

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:56.270
we would actually
be a contributor

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:58.800
in the Postgres space.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:01.200
And what we were now trying
to figure out is how many

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.550
such contributors would we have
building on top of DocumentDB.

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:08.940
And this was the smaller
cohort that were looking

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:12.030
to build managed
services of their own,

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:15.450
contribute to opensource
projects, and so on.

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:19.800
The second bucket, and this is
the revenue-driving bucket,

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:22.320
which is the product
side of things.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.960
And at this point, what we're
saying is these are companies.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.900
This is anybody that's building
a product of their own

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:29.970
that needs a database.

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:31.770
And at this point, it's not
just about contributions.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:32.820
You're actually looking for all

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:35.700
of the management functionality
that comes around it.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:38.310
So yes, you want a database,
but you don't want

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:39.990
to manage elasticity yourself.

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:43.170
You don't want to manage
storage and compute yourself.

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:45.600
You don't want to be
managing high availability

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:47.040
and disaster recovery.

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:48.390
You don't want to
be in the business

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:51.080
of injecting your own
security guardrails.

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:56.630
You want a service, a full-blown
product, not just a database,

00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:58.800
to manage all of this for you.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:02.370
And so these were the two clear
buckets that we had to look at

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:03.840
and say, how do
we cater to both?

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.680
But more importantly, how do we
build Azure DocumentDB on top

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:10.650
of the opensource project?

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:14.340
And really, in addition to
Citus, we also had a whole bunch

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:19.080
of other experts inhouse that we
were able to get learnings from.

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:20.520
Citus was one of them,

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:23.310
but we also have contributors
actively contributing

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:27.060
to opensource Postgres that
weren't just inspirations,

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:30.630
but were actually mentors to
us in terms of how we wanted

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:32.880
to manage both
project and product.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:35.190
TypeScript, for anyone
that's familiar

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:37.890
with the programming
language, has now become one

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:40.200
of the most popular
programming languages.

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:42.090
It's right up there
with Python and Node.

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:45.990
And that's an opensource
project that started inhouse,

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:49.170
and so a lot of feedback
and learnings from them.

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:53.010
And of course Visual Studio
with VS Code, it's now one

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:55.380
of the most popular
developer platforms.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.390
That's exactly the cohort

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:59.310
that we're looking
to cater to anyways.

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:03.720
So essentially, lots of
really good feedback.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:06.060
And this was actually something
that was interesting.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:09.690
So when we're looking to
build around opensource,

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:13.260
the key in all of this is we've
got a project on one side,

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:15.810
we've got a product
on the other side.

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:18.480
And now the idea was, how
do we measure success

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:23.280
of both while trying to
have both of them coexist?

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:24.960
And so when you think of
a product, when you think

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:27.870
of an Azure-managed service,
it's very clear you're looking

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:29.670
at the number of customers
you have, you're looking

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:32.070
at the number of clusters
you have, you're looking

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:35.610
at core counts and how much
storage I have under management.

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:40.440
How many revenue and
profits and there's all

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:44.370
of those typical business
metrics that come into play.

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:47.190
When you think of opensource,
it's actually very,

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:48.960
very different, right?

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.870
It's really you're
assessing the popularity

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:53.700
of an opensource project.

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:56.610
And there were three
very interesting metrics

00:11:56.610 --> 00:11:59.190
that we learned of, these
were all new to us.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:01.410
And one is called an elephant
factor, which is really,

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:03.030
we spoke about the
10 organizations

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:06.870
that had publicly supported
us and verbally supported us.

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:09.000
But how many of those
organizations are actually

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:10.440
contributing, right?

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:13.290
How many organizations
account for at least half

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:15.660
of the contributions
going into your project?

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:17.790
And the idea is you want
this number to be large.

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:19.830
So the more organizations
that are contributing

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:24.000
to at least 50% means you
have a larger community.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.610
And the fewer that
you have means a lot

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:28.770
of the contributions are
still heavily focused

00:12:28.770 --> 00:12:30.690
around one organization, right?

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:34.440
Which is still fine, but
there's not a lot of diversity

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:38.310
in the contributor pool
essentially is what this means.

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:40.200
The second one is
called a bus factor.

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:42.690
Now this one's as
ridiculous as it sounds.

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:44.400
What it really means is how many

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:47.520
of the vital contributors
can actually get hit

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:51.000
by a bus before the
project actually stalls?

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:53.490
So now this number, you
obviously want it to be as low

00:12:53.490 --> 00:12:56.520
as possible because that
actually means you have a lot

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:57.150
of diversity.

00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:58.680
There's a lot of people
contributing to it.

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:00.240
There's a lot of people that
are going to be able to pick

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:02.490
up the slack if someone
leaves the team,

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:05.580
if someone leaves the
organization, if someone decides

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:06.600
to do their own thing,

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.610
if someone decides
they've had enough.

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:11.640
So the idea is how
resilient is the project

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:15.360
to members actually leaving
the project itself.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:19.500
Now, the last one, this is
really more of a general health

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:22.230
of the project, which is
the heartbeat factor.

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:23.550
How many commits do you have?

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:25.140
How many pull
requests do you have?

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:26.670
How many releases do you have?

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:28.290
These could be minor
version releases.

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:30.330
These could be major
version releases.

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:34.320
So things of that nature, which
is obviously as this continues

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:38.100
to grow over time, what it means
is that there's more momentum.

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:39.390
It's not just one
of those projects

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:40.800
that nobody cares about.

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:43.620
This is something that the
community is truly building,

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:47.220
and it is something that we're
taking seriously, right?

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:49.350
And as you can see,
building a product

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:50.340
around this was very different

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:52.560
because the product is
ultimately going to be

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:56.670
about business metrics, but
the project is actually more

00:13:56.670 --> 00:14:00.990
about community engagement
and involvement metrics,

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:03.940
which is very different.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:07.110
What we wanted to
showcase here is

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:11.280
that both actually are
completely independent entities.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:15.270
So we do have a website,
DocumentDB.io, and if you go

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:18.120
to it, you'll actually see that
it lives completely outside.

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:19.920
It's community driven.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:21.030
You can actually contribute

00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:24.270
to the website's
repository yourself,

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:26.550
and we've actually gotten
things like a samples gallery.

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:28.470
These are all AI built.

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:31.110
We've got our own
skills.md that you can use

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:34.140
to build samples on
top of DocumentDB.

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:36.330
These can be vibe coded.

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:38.970
Essentially, it makes the
developer's life very,

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:40.320
very simple.

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:46.060
But it does live as an
independent entity of its own.

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:52.050
Now, the last step in this
process was really us joining

00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:53.430
the Linux Foundation.

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:54.840
Now, a lot of people
actually asked us,

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:57.540
what is your purpose
in all of this?

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:00.450
And the idea was when we
launched the project,

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:03.570
we got a lot of press, a
lot of great feedback,

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:05.400
a lot of users jumping in.

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:07.500
People wanted to try
it out immediately.

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:09.960
And a lot of Postgres
enthusiasts in particular,

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:14.040
which we were hoping to gain a
lot of traction from because

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:18.060
of how popular Postgres in
itself is, but then the idea

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:20.700
of it was why the foundation?

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:23.370
What is your angle here?

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:26.310
And our take on this was when
we think about something

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:27.540
like an elephant factor,

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:29.700
which is how many organizations
are actually contributing

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:31.170
to this, in the beginning,

00:15:31.170 --> 00:15:33.720
this was still very
heavily driven by us.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:36.570
And we didn't want
the community to feel

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:39.480
that at any point we could pull
the rug from underneath them.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:41.460
We could just say, oh,
sorry, you have to go

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:43.950
and use the
Azure-managed service

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:45.240
and we're no longer going

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:49.200
to support the opensource
project anymore.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:52.080
That could take a lot of
independence and power away

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:54.660
from the developers,
but that's something

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:58.110
that the developers would be
protected by just by virtue

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:00.630
of us joining a foundation,
especially a foundation

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:03.510
as credible as the
Linux Foundation,

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:06.300
where even if we then think
about the bus factor,

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:08.400
where if everybody
were to leave tomorrow

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:11.760
and suddenly there's absolutely
nobody looking at the project,

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:14.280
just by virtue of being
in the foundation,

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:17.490
developers have this sense
of comfort, the security

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:20.340
that the project itself
is not going to go away.

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:23.310
It will be picked up by the
foundation, and it will continue

00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:27.210
to live on and continue
to be supported

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:29.040
in whatever state it is.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:32.640
In the worst case, it'll just
be there, but it won't go away.

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:34.860
And it's something that the
developer community will

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:38.190
continue to be able to use
and build on top of and all

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:40.530
of the permissive components

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:43.270
of the project will
continue to exist.

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:49.440
Now, the real question in
all of this is why Postgres?

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:52.860
Now, we live in the era of AI.

00:16:52.860 --> 00:16:54.450
So when we're thinking
of building something

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:58.200
as fundamental as a database, we
don't have the luxury of time

00:16:58.200 --> 00:16:59.790
to go back and
start from scratch

00:16:59.790 --> 00:17:02.160
and build every single
component by itself.

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:05.220
Now, Postgres has been around
for a long, long time.

00:17:05.220 --> 00:17:07.200
It has solved a lot of problems.

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:09.900
And if you just look at the
popularity of Postgres,

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:11.850
there's over a million
developers worldwide.

00:17:11.850 --> 00:17:15.420
And I'm pretty sure this is a
massive, massive underestimate.

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:18.450
There's over 100,000
contributors

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:19.560
that Postgres has had.

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:22.950
And we have over bunch of them
here on our team as well.

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:25.050
If you just look at the
commits in the last year,

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:29.670
there's almost 45,000-plus
commits, 50k-plus GitHub stars,

00:17:29.670 --> 00:17:31.860
300 million-plus downloads.

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:34.530
All this to say it's an
incredibly popular opensource

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:37.200
database, and it's been the
fastest-growing opensource

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:40.470
database over the last decade.

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:42.240
And the beauty of Postgres,
and this is something

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:46.200
that we have leveraged in
Azure DocumentDB as well

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:51.030
as opensource DocumentDB, is
it's what we call extensible.

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:54.540
So if a new solution had to
be added in Postgres, yes,

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:55.590
you can add something
that's very,

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:57.990
very fundamental to
the engine itself.

00:17:57.990 --> 00:18:01.350
But you can add these
extensions on top of Postgres

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:03.360
that solve a whole
bunch of problems.

00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:06.060
If you're looking
to add replication

00:18:06.060 --> 00:18:08.310
and high availability,
we spoke about Citus.

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:11.730
Citus is actually a Postgres
extension that's built

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:12.600
on top of Postgres.

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:15.060
You wanted to build a
graph component to it.

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:18.900
That can be an extension on top
of Postgres, and it actually is.

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:21.420
We are in the era of AI.

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:24.330
So AI, when it comes to a
database, is all about RAG,

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:26.100
and it's all about
vector search.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:30.300
pgVector is an extension that's
built on top of Postgres

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:34.530
that allows users to do vector
search on top of their database

00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:40.850
for more semantically
meaningful results, right?

00:18:40.850 --> 00:18:42.920
And so what this now meant is

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:46.730
when we were building a NoSQL
database on top of Postgres

00:18:46.730 --> 00:18:49.490
as a foundation, as a
platform, we didn't have

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:51.770
to reinvent the wheel
when it came to indexing.

00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:53.060
We didn't have to
reinvent the wheel

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:55.370
when it came to
query performance.

00:18:55.370 --> 00:18:57.830
We didn't have to reinvent the
wheel when it came to throughput

00:18:57.830 --> 00:18:58.850
and high availability.

00:18:58.850 --> 00:19:00.080
A lot of these were solutions

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:02.960
and extensions that
already existed.

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:04.010
And so what we focused

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:09.420
on heavily was building the
Mongo query protocol on top

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:12.750
of Postgres that we could then
just funnel into Postgres

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:13.950
and leverage all the goodness

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:16.270
that Postgres brings
to the table.

00:19:18.170 --> 00:19:20.000
Great. So all of that

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:22.430
to say that's what our
opensource journey was like

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:24.680
and that's what our
decision-making process

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:27.740
into the opensource
project looked like,

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:30.860
why we chose Postgres, why we
chose the Linux Foundation

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:33.680
as a foundation to join, and
how we separated product

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:34.860
and project.

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:38.310
But now when we're looking at
the managed service in itself

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:39.990
with Azure DocumentDB
that we built

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:44.340
around opensource DocumentDB,
there were three primary pillars

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:45.930
that we had to target.

00:19:45.930 --> 00:19:47.970
The first one, and
the most obvious one,

00:19:47.970 --> 00:19:49.590
is we had to be cheaper.

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:53.190
So we had to provide the lowest
entry point, particularly

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:56.430
in a NoSQL space, in a
document database space,

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:59.040
in a Mongo API
compatibility space.

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:02.670
So TCO was something that
we focused on heavily.

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:08.070
And that is one of our biggest
claims to fame, if I can say so.

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:09.510
And then obviously
in addition to that,

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:12.420
that's the first entry point in
decision making is, okay, great,

00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:14.340
I need to find the
cheapest option.

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:15.600
That's fantastic.

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:20.280
But cheap and low TCO should
not compromise quality.

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:22.920
So how do we ensure
that we're also better?

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:24.750
Better in itself could
mean several things.

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:26.910
Better than the prior
versions of ourself.

00:20:26.910 --> 00:20:30.630
Better than competing products
out there in the market.

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:33.480
And how do we ensure that
we provide developers,

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:35.220
and in today's world, agents,

00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:39.540
the best possible
Mongo-compatible,

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:42.750
NoSQL database experience built

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:46.470
on opensource platforms
and projects?

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:49.460
And the last bucket in
all of that is, well,

00:20:49.460 --> 00:20:52.340
Azure through management
components might provide a

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:56.120
better solution, but
everything has to be fast.

00:20:56.120 --> 00:20:58.490
Everything has to keep
continuing to be faster

00:20:58.490 --> 00:20:59.780
and faster and faster.

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:03.270
So again, faster could mean
faster than our prior selves.

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:07.680
Faster could mean faster than
competing products out there,

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:10.440
faster than competing
projects out there.

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:13.440
And faster also means lower
latency and higher throughput.

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:16.770
So these were obviously the
three fundamental components

00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:20.790
that we had when building
this managed service.

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:23.700
So when we think about cheaper,
there were a few things

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:26.490
that we had to hit
right away, right?

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:30.990
When we built a managed
service around this,

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:33.690
the earliest decisions
that we made were storage

00:21:33.690 --> 00:21:35.820
and compute typically
go hand in hand.

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:38.610
It's hard to get rid
of one in the presence

00:21:38.610 --> 00:21:40.430
of the other and so on.

00:21:40.430 --> 00:21:44.270
But we could still separate
the cost of the two of them

00:21:44.270 --> 00:21:46.970
and not make them dependent.

00:21:46.970 --> 00:21:49.160
What we really mean by that
is there's a whole bunch

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:50.750
of competitive
products out there

00:21:50.750 --> 00:21:53.190
where if you say I
want a larger machine,

00:21:53.190 --> 00:21:56.160
that means you need a
larger storage disk.

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:59.760
Or you could say, they
might say the disk is only

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:03.450
of a certain size, and so
if you need more storage,

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:05.700
you cannot just
get a bigger disk.

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:08.790
You also have to throw another
machine on top of that

00:22:08.790 --> 00:22:11.430
to get your larger
storage component.

00:22:11.430 --> 00:22:15.210
And so this was something that
was a fairly large pain point

00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:18.270
that we wanted to
address for our users.

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:19.650
So when you look at this
example right here,

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:24.720
this is actually an example of a
real customer that moved 200TB

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:30.810
from a competing database
into Azure DocumentDB.

00:22:30.810 --> 00:22:33.480
So the first row that you see
there is actually what it looked

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:35.730
like for competitors
that say, well,

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:39.300
we're going to cap
you at 4000G or 4TB.

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:42.690
What that means is if I
have 200TB and I want

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:46.410
to leave enough headroom on
my storage layer for things

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:48.990
like compaction and so on,

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:53.880
that means I would need
100 compute nodes.

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:57.690
A hundred compute
nodes is insane.

00:22:57.690 --> 00:23:00.030
That's an absurd amount
of compute power

00:23:00.030 --> 00:23:02.430
that unless you're
building the most complex

00:23:02.430 --> 00:23:07.740
and the largest service in the
world, you really wouldn't need.

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:10.710
And so what we said was, why
do we need to cap you at 4TB?

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:13.140
What if we do 32TB?

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:15.150
And that's actually
what we have today.

00:23:15.150 --> 00:23:17.970
And of course the question
that might come up is, well,

00:23:17.970 --> 00:23:20.370
I am paying for eight times
the amount of storage,

00:23:20.370 --> 00:23:24.870
even if that means I've
dropped to effectively 1/8th

00:23:24.870 --> 00:23:27.090
of the amount of compute, which
is right around where we are

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:30.000
when you think about
13 nodes versus 100.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.350
But here's the difference.

00:23:31.350 --> 00:23:35.820
Storage increments are much
cheaper than compute increments.

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:40.980
So even though we've gone up in
storage by 8x, the drop by 8x

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:44.310
in compute saves us
a significant amount

00:23:44.310 --> 00:23:48.060
because compute ends up being
far more expensive than storage.

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:50.940
And the direction in which
we're headed is we are working

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:52.770
on having 64 terabyte disks.

00:23:52.770 --> 00:23:54.990
And eventually, this is
going to take a while

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:58.860
but we'll actually get
to 160 terabyte disks.

00:23:58.860 --> 00:24:01.470
And that means the number of
nodes that you now have is going

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:03.600
to be much, much fewer.

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:06.780
And these are still
six fairly beefy,

00:24:06.780 --> 00:24:09.390
fairly compute-intensive
machines.

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:12.930
And so you can still achieve
a lot with six machines.

00:24:12.930 --> 00:24:16.200
But if that's far fewer than you
would need, somewhere between 6

00:24:16.200 --> 00:24:19.620
and 12 or 13 is going to be
far, far within the range

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:22.560
of what you would actually need
for your service as opposed

00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:24.390
to something that's like 100,

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:26.700
which would just
be unnecessarily

00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:28.560
and massively overprovisioned.

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:30.540
So, separating the
cost of storage

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:34.050
and compute was a
significant driver for us.

00:24:34.050 --> 00:24:36.750
Now, the other piece in all
of this is when you look

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:38.970
at competitive
database products,

00:24:38.970 --> 00:24:42.870
we wanted the decision making
to be as simple as possible.

00:24:42.870 --> 00:24:45.150
There's a whole bunch
of components here

00:24:45.150 --> 00:24:47.850
that simply don't
exist in our product.

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:49.860
So we don't even
charge for them.

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:52.470
We eat up the cost and you
will not even see this

00:24:52.470 --> 00:24:54.360
in your invoice, right?

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:56.820
So we don't actually charge
for product support.

00:24:56.820 --> 00:24:58.770
It's a whole bunch of
products that will actually,

00:24:58.770 --> 00:25:02.670
despite you being an Azure
customer, also charge you

00:25:02.670 --> 00:25:05.880
for product support for
using their product.

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:08.880
It's not true for first-party
managed services,

00:25:08.880 --> 00:25:10.500
which is exactly what we are.

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:12.930
So, no additional cost there.

00:25:12.930 --> 00:25:14.760
We covered the license
when we were talking

00:25:14.760 --> 00:25:16.470
about opensource projects.

00:25:16.470 --> 00:25:17.880
Now, that's a very
different concept.

00:25:17.880 --> 00:25:20.940
That's just a license in terms
of how permissive it is for you

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:24.720
to use it and consume it
and build a product over it

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:26.370
and sell your product
that's built on top

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:27.990
of the project and so on.

00:25:27.990 --> 00:25:31.740
But when it comes to a license
to use the managed service,

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:33.330
we have no such thing.

00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:35.100
It's a subscription-based model.

00:25:35.100 --> 00:25:36.660
As soon as you tear
down your cluster,

00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:38.460
you're no longer being
billed for anything

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:39.900
because there's nothing.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:42.030
We don't charge for backups.

00:25:42.030 --> 00:25:43.560
So that example
that we just looked

00:25:43.560 --> 00:25:47.940
at with a 200-terabyte
workload, there is no cost

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:49.590
for backups per month.

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:52.830
That's an enormous
amount of savings.

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:56.040
So the more you bring in, the
more you will actually continue

00:25:56.040 --> 00:25:59.010
to save when you look
at the cheaper bucket

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:01.830
that we are trying
to hit as a product.

00:26:01.830 --> 00:26:02.940
No networking charges.

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:04.920
This is also a big, big thing.

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:08.040
Typically what happens is when
you're building an application

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:11.430
in Azure, your application runs
in a VNet, the database is

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:14.700
in another VNet, and
there's data that is going

00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:17.400
out of the database from
the database's VNet

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:21.030
and entering the customer's
application VNet.

00:26:21.030 --> 00:26:26.730
That data egress is typically
transferred onto a user

00:26:26.730 --> 00:26:28.590
and shows up on their invoice.

00:26:28.590 --> 00:26:31.380
Now, the more you pull out
of the database as VNet,

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:33.180
the more you end up
getting charged.

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:34.800
We said that doesn't make sense.

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:36.810
We're a first-party
Azure product.

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:39.120
We will not charge
our customers.

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:42.120
This is at least
20% of an invoice

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:45.780
from a competitive product that
you would just see wiped away.

00:26:45.780 --> 00:26:47.310
And the last piece, this
is really something

00:26:47.310 --> 00:26:48.720
that we just wanted to call out.

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:50.880
Obviously, when you're
building any system,

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:53.730
whether it's agent-based,
AI-based, developer-based,

00:26:53.730 --> 00:26:56.580
human-based, you always want to
have some sort of telemetry.

00:26:56.580 --> 00:26:59.190
You need to know that
your system is healthy,

00:26:59.190 --> 00:27:00.630
it's working fine.

00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:03.090
If it isn't healthy, do I
have a mitigation step that's

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:04.260
kicked in?

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:05.880
And so the database
being at the bottom

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:09.270
of the stack obviously plays
a huge role in all of that.

00:27:09.270 --> 00:27:12.060
There's no cost associated with
our metrics, with our charts.

00:27:12.060 --> 00:27:13.470
It's completely free.

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:17.580
We believe that you shouldn't
have to pay for observability

00:27:17.580 --> 00:27:19.870
for something that
you need to use.

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:23.700
And now here's an
interesting customer story.

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:26.670
And this was actually a
customer that migrated from DB2

00:27:26.670 --> 00:27:33.060
on premise to Azure DocumentDB
in Azure, obviously.

00:27:33.060 --> 00:27:40.020
This was a 262-terabyte
migration from DB2 to Azure.

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:43.230
And the biggest selling point
in all of this was the fact

00:27:43.230 --> 00:27:46.110
that we had 32-terabyte disks.

00:27:46.110 --> 00:27:50.250
And when we compared our
cost to what DB2 ended

00:27:50.250 --> 00:27:53.790
up costing them on-prem in
addition to the backups

00:27:53.790 --> 00:27:57.660
that they were maintaining,
data transfer costs,

00:27:57.660 --> 00:28:00.030
their own data center costs,
and the compute machines

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:02.970
that they needed, they
ended up saving close

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:09.540
to $9 million a year, not just
over their current expense,

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:14.100
but even when compared to
competitive products that were

00:28:14.100 --> 00:28:18.270
in the NoSQL
Mongo-compatible space.

00:28:18.270 --> 00:28:21.270
So this ended up being
an absolute no-brainer,

00:28:21.270 --> 00:28:24.270
and large disks and the
Azure-managed components

00:28:24.270 --> 00:28:25.620
around it, particularly
everything

00:28:25.620 --> 00:28:29.010
that Azure DocumentDB provided
from a TCO standpoint,

00:28:29.010 --> 00:28:32.910
ended up being a
no-brainer for them.

00:28:32.910 --> 00:28:34.020
Now, here's another example.

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:35.430
In this case, it wasn't DB2.

00:28:35.430 --> 00:28:38.490
This was actually a
customer moving from HBase

00:28:38.490 --> 00:28:40.230
into Azure DocumentDB.

00:28:40.230 --> 00:28:41.220
And this was fascinating.

00:28:41.220 --> 00:28:45.300
So you obviously have
your obvious components

00:28:45.300 --> 00:28:47.670
like compute and storage.

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:50.280
And sure, they're
reasonably close.

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:54.420
We ended up being a good amount
cheaper in storage as well.

00:28:54.420 --> 00:28:59.100
But what's interesting here
is you also have a licensing

00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:01.260
component, and we
just covered that.

00:29:01.260 --> 00:29:04.080
In our case we've
got no license,

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:07.830
and we didn't even factor in
the cost of backups on HBase

00:29:07.830 --> 00:29:11.220
because we didn't even see it
on this customer's invoice.

00:29:11.220 --> 00:29:14.220
And we're actually
ignoring the fact

00:29:14.220 --> 00:29:15.780
that that backups
would have shown up.

00:29:15.780 --> 00:29:17.280
But even in the absence of that,

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:21.720
what we saw was a customer cut
their bill in more than half.

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:26.520
And more than anything, yes,
there was a cost play here.

00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:30.660
But the biggest, the most
meaningful impact was the fact

00:29:30.660 --> 00:29:32.700
that they had to make
fewer decisions, right?

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:34.740
You're only thinking of
the size of your machine

00:29:34.740 --> 00:29:36.090
and the size of your disk.

00:29:36.090 --> 00:29:37.470
You don't have to
worry about licensing.

00:29:37.470 --> 00:29:38.730
You don't have to
worry about backups.

00:29:38.730 --> 00:29:40.020
You don't have to worry
about networking.

00:29:40.020 --> 00:29:42.420
You don't have to worry about
a whole bunch of other things

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:45.240
that are going to require some
kind of rocket science for you

00:29:45.240 --> 00:29:48.360
to actually figure out how much
it will cost you at the end.

00:29:48.360 --> 00:29:50.940
There's just two things,
and two things only.

00:29:50.940 --> 00:29:52.320
Here's how much it
will come out to.

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:55.300
It's predictable, and
that's the end of it.

00:29:56.310 --> 00:29:59.700
Now, the next step, once we
solved the cheaper bucket,

00:29:59.700 --> 00:30:03.780
was obviously addressing
the faster bucket, right?

00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:06.930
And this was a very
interesting journey for us.

00:30:06.930 --> 00:30:10.860
So by virtue of being built in
Azure as a managed service,

00:30:10.860 --> 00:30:13.860
there's obviously components
in the Azure ecosystem

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:16.680
that we can leverage
for our own product.

00:30:16.680 --> 00:30:20.460
And premium managed disks, SSD
v2, which has been all the rage

00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:23.430
over the last year at the
very least but has started

00:30:23.430 --> 00:30:26.580
to become a lot more
mainstream now,

00:30:26.580 --> 00:30:28.380
essentially came to the rescue.

00:30:28.380 --> 00:30:29.970
In this case, we
actually said, well,

00:30:29.970 --> 00:30:34.230
let's compare our former
selves to our current selves,

00:30:34.230 --> 00:30:36.450
and then see how much
better we're doing.

00:30:36.450 --> 00:30:39.150
And obviously this has
to make sense not just

00:30:39.150 --> 00:30:40.410
from a performance perspective,

00:30:40.410 --> 00:30:43.590
but this should not cost
the user anything more.

00:30:43.590 --> 00:30:45.570
So it doesn't make any sense
if we're saying, well,

00:30:45.570 --> 00:30:48.180
we can actually give you 12x
the performance that we used

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:50.520
to have, but we actually
want you to pay 12x

00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:51.870
of what you used to pay as well.

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:54.300
Nobody's ever going
to go for that.

00:30:54.300 --> 00:30:57.570
In the end, what we said was, if
we look at where we used to be,

00:30:57.570 --> 00:31:00.570
the biggest selling
point with SSD v2 is

00:31:00.570 --> 00:31:04.440
that it can get you far more
IOPS, far more bandwidth,

00:31:04.440 --> 00:31:06.240
and you don't have to
keep provisioning larger

00:31:06.240 --> 00:31:07.440
and larger disks.

00:31:07.440 --> 00:31:08.580
So we said that's something

00:31:08.580 --> 00:31:12.030
that our users should
actually benefit from.

00:31:12.030 --> 00:31:15.780
In that example where we said
our competitors actually cap you

00:31:15.780 --> 00:31:19.680
out at 4TB while we
go up to 32TB today,

00:31:19.680 --> 00:31:20.910
we wanted to make the same claim

00:31:20.910 --> 00:31:23.010
about performance
as well, right?

00:31:23.010 --> 00:31:27.360
In a prior scenario, we used
to be capped at 20,000 IOPS

00:31:27.360 --> 00:31:30.180
and you had to move to the
largest possible disk.

00:31:30.180 --> 00:31:32.430
But today what we're
saying is that we can go

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:34.950
up to 80,000 IOPS,
you don't have to go

00:31:34.950 --> 00:31:39.990
to the largest possible disk,
and we actually eat up the cost

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:42.720
of those IOPS ourselves.

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:45.510
So effectively what that
means is in all of this,

00:31:45.510 --> 00:31:49.740
you've now gotten a
12x performance boost

00:31:49.740 --> 00:31:54.090
at no configuration
change, no scale up,

00:31:54.090 --> 00:31:58.470
and we've truly decoupled
performance from the size

00:31:58.470 --> 00:32:00.360
of your configuration.

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:03.960
This was a massive,
massive, massive reduction.

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:07.950
So in the past, what this would
have meant is you had to start

00:32:07.950 --> 00:32:13.740
at $280 a month and go
up to $4,500 a month.

00:32:13.740 --> 00:32:17.700
What we're now saying is
you start at $280 a month,

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:22.530
stay at $280 a month, and
you get the same performance

00:32:22.530 --> 00:32:25.860
that you previously would
have needed $4,500 for.

00:32:25.860 --> 00:32:28.890
And so this was a pretty,
pretty significant boost.

00:32:28.890 --> 00:32:32.400
And it's faster because the IOPS
essentially mean your writes are

00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:34.890
faster, your reads are
faster, your latencies

00:32:34.890 --> 00:32:36.780
on queries are faster.

00:32:36.780 --> 00:32:38.760
So there's a lot of benefits.

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:41.670
And because this was an
internal optimization,

00:32:41.670 --> 00:32:46.440
we felt that you should not be
charged as a user for this.

00:32:46.440 --> 00:32:48.150
And here's something else
that was interesting.

00:32:48.150 --> 00:32:50.520
We did an experiment recently.

00:32:50.520 --> 00:32:53.580
And with SSD v2, we were able

00:32:53.580 --> 00:33:00.120
to achieve 1 million writes
per second on a single node.

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:02.520
And the details of it are
really what we've put in here.

00:33:02.520 --> 00:33:03.930
We had a certain machine.

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:07.230
It was a 32-core machine.

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:08.250
The disk was small.

00:33:08.250 --> 00:33:10.290
This was just a 2TB disk.

00:33:10.290 --> 00:33:13.890
In the past, we would
have needed a 32TB disk

00:33:13.890 --> 00:33:15.930
to get to 20,000 IOPS.

00:33:15.930 --> 00:33:18.240
In this particular case,
we had a 2TB disk,

00:33:18.240 --> 00:33:21.300
and we got to 80,000 IOPS.

00:33:21.300 --> 00:33:23.430
Let's just think about
that for a second.

00:33:23.430 --> 00:33:30.510
And we achieved a million writes
per second on a single node.

00:33:30.510 --> 00:33:35.490
This is a huge, huge,
huge milestone.

00:33:35.490 --> 00:33:38.160
And more so than anything, when
people look at this, again,

00:33:38.160 --> 00:33:42.450
the usual question that comes
up is, well, are you asking me

00:33:42.450 --> 00:33:46.500
to provision an absurd number
of machines, an absurd amount

00:33:46.500 --> 00:33:49.200
of compute to achieve a
million writes per second?

00:33:49.200 --> 00:33:51.000
And so the best way
to quantify this is

00:33:51.000 --> 00:33:55.830
to actually translate this
into transactions per dollar.

00:33:55.830 --> 00:34:00.450
And we're almost at 200,000
transactions per dollar.

00:34:00.450 --> 00:34:07.230
If we compare a very similar
setup on a competitor product,

00:34:07.230 --> 00:34:12.360
what we can actually see
is they get to only 80%

00:34:12.360 --> 00:34:13.770
of what we're able to.

00:34:13.770 --> 00:34:14.880
And we're also cheaper.

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:18.000
And so what that means is even
though we're looking at a 20%

00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:21.390
to 25% difference in the actual
throughput, we're looking

00:34:21.390 --> 00:34:26.190
at a nearly 2x difference
in transactions per dollar.

00:34:26.190 --> 00:34:29.130
And all this to say, by the
way, we still had headroom.

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:31.620
So we could have gone
beyond a million.

00:34:31.620 --> 00:34:34.560
But a million is where we were
able to get truly sustained

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:38.220
and consistent results.

00:34:38.220 --> 00:34:43.320
And with that, we have a demo
to show the next piece in all

00:34:43.320 --> 00:34:46.050
of this, which is
the better bucket.

00:34:46.050 --> 00:34:49.050
And the better bucket is all
about how we're now able

00:34:49.050 --> 00:34:52.500
to achieve multicloud
replication.

00:34:52.500 --> 00:34:55.950
Because we are built on
an opensource foundation,

00:34:55.950 --> 00:34:57.450
and as we spoke about earlier,

00:34:57.450 --> 00:35:00.750
the opensource foundation
essentially means we can run

00:35:00.750 --> 00:35:03.120
within Azure, we can
run outside of Azure,

00:35:03.120 --> 00:35:05.580
and we've got a managed
service in Azure.

00:35:05.580 --> 00:35:09.450
But what we are able to do is
actually connect the managed

00:35:09.450 --> 00:35:13.350
component in Azure to pieces
outside of Azure as well

00:35:13.350 --> 00:35:15.660
in a different Cloud,
in a hybrid Cloud,

00:35:15.660 --> 00:35:17.100
and that's essentially
what we are going

00:35:17.100 --> 00:35:19.540
to showcase right here.

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:24.600
Hello. In this demo, we
will showcase multicloud

00:35:24.600 --> 00:35:27.420
functionality for DocumentDB
using the opensource engine

00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:28.890
coupled with the
Kubernetes operator

00:35:28.890 --> 00:35:31.350
for the database provision
across Azure, AWS,

00:35:31.350 --> 00:35:34.560
and GCP Kubernetes clusters.

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:37.290
You can see here on the plugin
that the state of the system

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:39.990
at the start is that
Azure hosts the primary

00:35:39.990 --> 00:35:43.440
and it replicates
to GCP and AWS.

00:35:43.440 --> 00:35:45.060
All three are using Kubernetes

00:35:45.060 --> 00:35:48.360
with our opensource operator
and database engine.

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:52.230
There's one instance each for
our replicas on AWS and GCP,

00:35:52.230 --> 00:35:55.620
while there are three
instances on Azure.

00:35:55.620 --> 00:35:58.050
This setup ensures globally
strong data consistency

00:35:58.050 --> 00:35:59.880
and local high availability.

00:35:59.880 --> 00:36:01.860
Using our VS Code
extension for DocumentDB,

00:36:01.860 --> 00:36:04.110
we have on the left the
three separate connections.

00:36:04.110 --> 00:36:05.040
Those each connect

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:07.380
to the cluster individually
on each cloud.

00:36:07.380 --> 00:36:09.930
But in our application, we use
a global connection string,

00:36:09.930 --> 00:36:12.150
which points to the primary
and automatically changes

00:36:12.150 --> 00:36:13.650
when a failover is
triggered to point

00:36:13.650 --> 00:36:15.690
to the new primary
region for the database.

00:36:15.690 --> 00:36:18.440
So right now, it'll
point to AKS.

00:36:18.440 --> 00:36:20.450
But after we do our
promotion later in the demo,

00:36:20.450 --> 00:36:22.640
that connection string will
start pointing to AWS.

00:36:22.640 --> 00:36:25.250
We're inserting data
into DocumentDB on AKS.

00:36:25.250 --> 00:36:27.110
But I'm pulling up the
collection on GCP,

00:36:27.110 --> 00:36:29.850
and you'll be able to see the
records are being replicated

00:36:29.850 --> 00:36:34.050
to GCP and also to AWS.

00:36:34.050 --> 00:36:36.300
With our global strong
consistency setup,

00:36:36.300 --> 00:36:37.980
any database insert
operation made

00:36:37.980 --> 00:36:40.410
to the primary will only
return a successful response

00:36:40.410 --> 00:36:42.870
to the client if the ingest
data has been acknowledged

00:36:42.870 --> 00:36:45.720
by both the primary and at
least one remote replica.

00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:48.930
This ensures that if the primary
goes down, all writes confirmed

00:36:48.930 --> 00:36:51.630
by DocumentDB can reliably
be assumed to be safe

00:36:51.630 --> 00:36:53.880
with absolutely no data loss.

00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:57.540
I'm going to pause for a moment
now to allow some of the inserts

00:36:57.540 --> 00:37:00.630
to propagate to our data
collection through Prometheus

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:03.570
and Grafana so that we
can view the chart.

00:37:03.570 --> 00:37:06.570
Now that that's done, you can
see all the writes on Azure here

00:37:06.570 --> 00:37:11.280
in green, and we can now promote
AWS to be our new primary.

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:14.190
The promotion that we're doing
here is a managed promotion.

00:37:14.190 --> 00:37:17.190
So the primary on Azure
is first demoted,

00:37:17.190 --> 00:37:21.420
rights are all committed, and
then the AWS replica is promoted

00:37:21.420 --> 00:37:23.070
to be the new primary.

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:26.190
A similar process is triggered
when a regional failover occurs,

00:37:26.190 --> 00:37:28.500
wherein the replica is
promoted after we lose access

00:37:28.500 --> 00:37:30.990
to the primary, and data loss
would still be prevented

00:37:30.990 --> 00:37:33.810
by our global strong guarantee.

00:37:33.810 --> 00:37:36.120
Now, I'll let this run
for a while as well

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:39.000
so that we can see the failover
in the Grafana charts.

00:37:39.000 --> 00:37:41.370
As you can see here, because
it was a managed failover,

00:37:41.370 --> 00:37:44.040
all pending writes
are committed on AKS,

00:37:44.040 --> 00:37:46.410
so you get that lower level,
and then our rights start

00:37:46.410 --> 00:37:50.850
to increase on AWS as the
failover is over, seen in green.

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:53.220
As you can see, there was only
a small blip for the failover

00:37:53.220 --> 00:37:55.350
and no application site
intervention was needed

00:37:55.350 --> 00:37:58.430
to restore availability
on a different cloud.

00:37:58.430 --> 00:38:01.460
And there we have it, a globally
distributed setup for DocumentDB

00:38:01.460 --> 00:38:03.350
across three clouds,
with a quick,

00:38:03.350 --> 00:38:06.990
fully managed failover
and zero data loss.

00:38:08.460 --> 00:38:12.120
So in that demo, what we
showcased was multicloud

00:38:12.120 --> 00:38:14.070
replication for
Azure DocumentDB,

00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:16.950
where you have Azure DocumentDB
obviously running in Azure,

00:38:16.950 --> 00:38:22.490
and we're able to highlight
replication to AWS and GCP

00:38:22.490 --> 00:38:24.410
with absolutely no data loss

00:38:24.410 --> 00:38:27.770
and multicloud failover
functionality as well.

00:38:27.770 --> 00:38:33.060
Now, in this demo, what we
have is a hybrid cloud setup

00:38:33.060 --> 00:38:36.000
where the entire
purpose is the ability

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:39.540
to run opensource DocumentDB
in a hybrid cloud,

00:38:39.540 --> 00:38:42.720
and this could be on-prem,
this could be on IaaS,

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:47.400
and then connect that
to Azure DocumentDB.

00:38:47.400 --> 00:38:49.560
And also provide functionality

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:53.130
where your opensource version
could continue to exist.

00:38:53.130 --> 00:38:55.470
So the hybrid cloud
running on-prem

00:38:55.470 --> 00:38:57.840
or wherever it is could
continue to exist even

00:38:57.840 --> 00:39:02.430
when the replication pipe to
Azure let's say is broken

00:39:02.430 --> 00:39:04.320
and steady state continues.

00:39:04.320 --> 00:39:06.840
And when that replication
pipe picks up again

00:39:06.840 --> 00:39:09.060
and connectivity has
been re-established,

00:39:09.060 --> 00:39:13.230
the sync simply picks up
from where it last left off,

00:39:13.230 --> 00:39:14.070
and we're right back

00:39:14.070 --> 00:39:16.320
to replicating into
the cloud again.

00:39:16.320 --> 00:39:19.680
So with that, here's the demo.

00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:22.350
In this video, we will
highlight sync replication

00:39:22.350 --> 00:39:24.090
in a hybrid cloud deployment

00:39:24.090 --> 00:39:27.780
for Azure DocumentDB using
opensource DocumentDB running

00:39:27.780 --> 00:39:29.820
on-prem using the
Kubernetes operator,

00:39:29.820 --> 00:39:31.350
which is the first
connection that you see

00:39:31.350 --> 00:39:34.110
in the top left corner of
our VS Code extension,

00:39:34.110 --> 00:39:37.350
to Azure DocumentDB
running in the cloud.

00:39:37.350 --> 00:39:40.530
What you see in the terminal
output is a sync service

00:39:40.530 --> 00:39:44.490
that has been triggered
to keep the two in sync.

00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:48.030
Both have no documents at the
moment, and we will start

00:39:48.030 --> 00:39:52.350
by making a single
document insert on-prem.

00:39:52.350 --> 00:39:55.200
And we will then validate
that the sync service,

00:39:55.200 --> 00:39:57.870
after persisting the
document locally,

00:39:57.870 --> 00:40:01.770
replicated the change
to Azure as well.

00:40:01.770 --> 00:40:03.810
And when we click on
Azure DocumentDB,

00:40:03.810 --> 00:40:06.810
we see the new document
has made its way here.

00:40:06.810 --> 00:40:12.090
Next, we will make a change to
update the document on-prem.

00:40:12.090 --> 00:40:16.080
We will make changes to
two fields specifically.

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:19.950
And once again, after
persisting the update locally,

00:40:19.950 --> 00:40:23.340
we now want to validate that
the sync service was also able

00:40:23.340 --> 00:40:27.750
to replicate the change to
this document to Azure.

00:40:27.750 --> 00:40:29.490
And when we click on
Azure DocumentDB,

00:40:29.490 --> 00:40:32.280
we see both fields
changed here as well.

00:40:32.280 --> 00:40:33.720
Now, the last step that we want

00:40:33.720 --> 00:40:38.970
to validate is steady state
operations continue undisturbed

00:40:38.970 --> 00:40:42.330
even when the replication
sync service is down.

00:40:42.330 --> 00:40:45.420
So to do this, we've stopped
the replication service,

00:40:45.420 --> 00:40:50.490
we go back in to our on-prem
deployment of Azure DocumentDB.

00:40:50.490 --> 00:40:52.140
And what we want
to do is we want

00:40:52.140 --> 00:40:56.670
to make three document inserts,
and we want to validate

00:40:56.670 --> 00:41:00.960
that there were no errors in
persisting the data locally.

00:41:00.960 --> 00:41:03.570
And of course then
we want to validate

00:41:03.570 --> 00:41:06.990
that the changes did not
make it to Azure DocumentDB

00:41:06.990 --> 00:41:08.850
because our sync
service is down.

00:41:08.850 --> 00:41:11.250
And here we can see that
there was only one document

00:41:11.250 --> 00:41:14.640
in Azure while there
were four on-prem.

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:18.300
We'll then go back,
restart the sync service.

00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:22.680
And once it's restarted and we
go back into Azure DocumentDB,

00:41:22.680 --> 00:41:25.170
we should see the three
additional documents have now

00:41:25.170 --> 00:41:27.490
made their way here as well.

00:41:31.970 --> 00:41:33.570
And that's all we have today.

00:41:33.570 --> 00:41:35.760
Thank you for being a
part of my session.

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:38.820
Thank you for listening
to our long winding story

00:41:38.820 --> 00:41:43.560
on how we built Azure DocumentDB
on top of opensource foundations

00:41:43.560 --> 00:41:46.110
and on top of an
opensource project.

00:41:46.110 --> 00:41:47.880
What we have here are
a couple of links.

00:41:47.880 --> 00:41:50.730
As I mentioned, we have
our opensource project

00:41:50.730 --> 00:41:52.830
as an independent entity.

00:41:52.830 --> 00:41:56.160
And if you'd like to stay
up-to-date with events,

00:41:56.160 --> 00:41:59.790
with updates, anything to do
with opensource DocumentDB,

00:41:59.790 --> 00:42:02.220
you can go to documentdb.io.

00:42:02.220 --> 00:42:05.430
And of course at the bottom is
a link to our managed service

00:42:05.430 --> 00:42:09.510
in Azure to keep up-to-date on
new features, new components

00:42:09.510 --> 00:42:14.250
to our product, performance
improvements, TCO benefits,

00:42:14.250 --> 00:42:17.430
anything that continues to
change over time, that's exactly

00:42:17.430 --> 00:42:19.110
where you can find all of it.

00:42:19.110 --> 00:42:22.090
And thank you for listening.
